# TanStack Query v5 and Zustand Best Practices for React Native Expo

## QueryClient Setup and Configuration

### ✅ DO: Single QueryClient Instance
Always create a single QueryClient instance and reuse it across your application. Never create QueryClient instances inside React components.

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Single instance in a dedicated file
// services/queryClient.ts
import { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query'

export const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
      gcTime: 10 * 60 * 1000, // 10 minutes (formerly cacheTime)
      retry: (failureCount, error) => {
        // Don't retry on 4xx errors
        if (error instanceof Error && 'status' in error && typeof error.status === 'number') {
          if (error.status >= 400 && error.status < 500) return false
        }
        return failureCount < 3
      },
      networkMode: 'online', // React Native should handle network states
    },
    mutations: {
      retry: false, // Mutations typically shouldn't retry
      networkMode: 'online',
    },
  },
})
```

```typescript
// ❌ Bad: Creating QueryClient in component
function App() {
  const queryClient = new QueryClient() // Creates new instance on every render!
  return (
    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
      <Home />
    </QueryClientProvider>
  )
}
```

### ✅ DO: Configure React Native Specific Options
For React Native, configure network mode and focus refetching appropriately:

```typescript
export const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      networkMode: 'online', // Wait for network before fetching
      refetchOnWindowFocus: false, // Disable on mobile (causes issues)
      refetchOnReconnect: true, // Enable when connection returns
      refetchOnMount: true, // Usually want fresh data on mount
    },
  },
})
```

## Query Keys and Structure

### ✅ DO: Use Consistent Query Key Patterns
Establish consistent patterns for query keys to enable proper caching and invalidation:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Consistent, hierarchical keys
const queryKeys = {
  // Base keys
  users: ['users'] as const,
  posts: ['posts'] as const,
  comments: ['comments'] as const,

  // Specific variations
  user: (id: string) => [...queryKeys.users, id] as const,
  userPosts: (userId: string) => [...queryKeys.users, userId, 'posts'] as const,
  post: (id: string) => [...queryKeys.posts, id] as const,
  postComments: (postId: string) => [...queryKeys.posts, postId, 'comments'] as const,
} as const

// Usage
const { data: user } = useQuery({
  queryKey: queryKeys.user(userId),
  queryFn: () => fetchUser(userId),
})

const { data: posts } = useQuery({
  queryKey: queryKeys.userPosts(userId),
  queryFn: () => fetchUserPosts(userId),
})
```

### ✅ DO: Include All Dependencies in Query Keys
Ensure query keys include all variables used in the query function:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: All dependencies in key
const { data } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['posts', userId, status, page], // Includes all variables
  queryFn: () => fetchPosts({ userId, status, page }),
})

// ❌ Bad: Missing dependencies
const { data } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['posts'], // Missing userId, status, page
  queryFn: () => fetchPosts({ userId, status, page }), // Uses variables not in key
})
```

## Data Fetching and Mutations

### ✅ DO: Use TypeScript for Better Type Safety
Always use proper TypeScript types for queries and mutations:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Proper typing
interface User {
  id: string
  name: string
  email: string
}

interface CreateUserData {
  name: string
  email: string
}

const useUser = (userId: string) => {
  return useQuery<User, Error>({
    queryKey: queryKeys.user(userId),
    queryFn: (): Promise<User> => fetchUser(userId),
  })
}

const useCreateUser = () => {
  return useMutation<User, Error, CreateUserData>({
    mutationFn: (data: CreateUserData) => createUser(data),
    onSuccess: (data) => {
      // Invalidate and refetch user list
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.users })
    },
  })
}
```

### ✅ DO: Implement Optimistic Updates for Better UX
Use optimistic updates for mutations that improve perceived performance:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Optimistic updates
const useUpdateUser = () => {
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: updateUser,
    onMutate: async (newUser) => {
      // Cancel outgoing refetches
      await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.user(newUser.id) })

      // Snapshot previous value
      const previousUser = queryClient.getQueryData(queryKeys.user(newUser.id))

      // Optimistically update
      queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.user(newUser.id), newUser)

      // Return context for rollback
      return { previousUser, newUser }
    },
    onError: (err, newUser, context) => {
      // Rollback on error
      if (context?.previousUser) {
        queryClient.setQueryData(queryKeys.user(newUser.id), context.previousUser)
      }
    },
    onSettled: (data, error, newUser) => {
      // Always refetch after error or success
      queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: queryKeys.user(newUser.id) })
    },
  })
}
```

## Zustand Store Design

### ✅ DO: Use Slices Pattern for Complex Stores
Organize complex state using the slices pattern for better maintainability:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Slices pattern
// stores/authSlice.ts
export interface AuthSlice {
  user: User | null
  isAuthenticated: boolean
  login: (credentials: LoginCredentials) => Promise<void>
  logout: () => void
}

export const createAuthSlice: StateCreator<AuthSlice> = (set) => ({
  user: null,
  isAuthenticated: false,
  login: async (credentials) => {
    const user = await loginUser(credentials)
    set({ user, isAuthenticated: true })
  },
  logout: () => {
    set({ user: null, isAuthenticated: false })
  },
})

// stores/index.ts
export const useStore = create<AuthSlice & OtherSlices>((...a) => ({
  ...createAuthSlice(...a),
  ...createOtherSlice(...a),
}))
```

### ✅ DO: Use Selectors for Performance Optimization
Always use selectors to prevent unnecessary re-renders:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Selective subscriptions
const UserProfile = () => {
  const user = useStore((state) => state.user)
  const isAuthenticated = useStore((state) => state.isAuthenticated)
  const logout = useStore((state) => state.logout)

  return (
    <View>
      {isAuthenticated ? (
        <>
          <Text>Welcome, {user?.name}</Text>
          <Button title="Logout" onPress={logout} />
        </>
      ) : (
        <Text>Please login</Text>
      )}
    </View>
  )
}

// ❌ Bad: Subscribing to entire store
const UserProfile = () => {
  const store = useStore() // Subscribes to everything!
  // ...
}
```

### ✅ DO: Implement Proper Immutable Updates
Always use immutable update patterns, especially for complex objects:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Immutable updates
const useProfileStore = create((set) => ({
  profile: {
    name: '',
    settings: {
      theme: 'light',
      notifications: true,
    },
  },
  updateProfile: (updates: Partial<typeof profile>) =>
    set((state) => ({
      profile: { ...state.profile, ...updates },
    })),
  updateSettings: (settings: Partial<typeof profile.settings>) =>
    set((state) => ({
      profile: {
        ...state.profile,
        settings: { ...state.profile.settings, ...settings },
      },
    })),
}))
```

## Integration Patterns

### ✅ DO: Use Zustand for Client State, Query for Server State
Follow the separation of concerns - Zustand for UI state, TanStack Query for server state:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Clear separation
// Zustand store for UI state
const useUISlice = create((set) => ({
  sidebarOpen: false,
  selectedTab: 'home',
  setSidebarOpen: (open: boolean) => set({ sidebarOpen: open }),
  setSelectedTab: (tab: string) => set({ selectedTab: tab }),
}))

// TanStack Query for server data
const usePosts = (userId?: string) => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: userId ? queryKeys.userPosts(userId) : queryKeys.posts,
    queryFn: () => (userId ? fetchUserPosts(userId) : fetchPosts()),
  })
}

### ✅ DO: Handle Loading and Error States Properly
Implement comprehensive loading and error handling:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Comprehensive state handling
const PostsList = () => {
  const { data: posts, isLoading, error, refetch } = usePosts()

  if (isLoading) {
    return <ActivityIndicator size="large" />
  }

  if (error) {
    return (
      <View>
        <Text>Error loading posts</Text>
        <Button title="Retry" onPress={() => refetch()} />
      </View>
    )
  }

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={posts}
      renderItem={({ item }) => <PostItem post={item} />}
      onRefresh={refetch}
      refreshing={isLoading}
    />
  )
}
```

## React Native Specific Considerations

### ✅ DO: Handle Network State Changes
Implement proper network state handling for React Native:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Network-aware queries
import NetInfo from '@react-native-community/netinfo'

const useNetworkStore = create((set) => ({
  isOnline: true,
  setOnlineStatus: (isOnline: boolean) => set({ isOnline }),
}))

// Listen to network changes
useEffect(() => {
  const unsubscribe = NetInfo.addEventListener(state => {
    useNetworkStore.getState().setOnlineStatus(state.isConnected ?? false)
  })
  return unsubscribe
}, [])

// Use in queries
const { data } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['data'],
  queryFn: fetchData,
  enabled: useNetworkStore.getState().isOnline,
  networkMode: 'online',
})
```

### ✅ DO: Implement Background Fetch for Offline Support
Handle offline scenarios gracefully:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Offline-first approach
const useTodos = () => {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['todos'],
    queryFn: fetchTodos,
    networkMode: 'offlineFirst', // Use cache when offline
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // Consider data fresh for 5 minutes
    gcTime: 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, // Keep in cache for 24 hours
  })
}

// Store for offline actions
const useOfflineStore = create((set) => ({
  pendingActions: [],
  addPendingAction: (action: any) =>
    set((state) => ({
      pendingActions: [...state.pendingActions, action],
    })),
}))
```

## Performance Optimization

### ✅ DO: Use React.memo and useMemo for Expensive Operations
Optimize component renders:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Memoized components
const PostItem = React.memo(({ post }: { post: Post }) => {
  const formattedDate = useMemo(() =>
    formatDate(post.createdAt), [post.createdAt]
  )

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{post.title}</Text>
      <Text>{formattedDate}</Text>
    </View>
  )
})
```

### ✅ DO: Implement Proper Dependency Management
Use exhaustive-deps and stable references:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Stable references
const usePostFilters = () => {
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ status: 'all', category: '' })

  const filteredPosts = useMemo(() =>
    filterPosts(posts, filters), [posts, filters]
  )

  return { filters, setFilters, filteredPosts }
}

// ❌ Bad: Unstable dependencies
const { data: posts } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['posts', filters], // filters object changes on every render
  queryFn: () => fetchPosts(filters),
})
```

## Error Handling and Debugging

### ✅ DO: Implement Global Error Boundaries
Set up proper error handling:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Error boundaries for query errors
const QueryErrorBoundary = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary
      fallback={({ error, resetError }) => (
        <View>
          <Text>Something went wrong</Text>
          <Button title="Retry" onPress={resetError} />
        </View>
      )}
    >
      {children}
    </ErrorBoundary>
  )
}

// Usage
<QueryErrorBoundary>
  <App />
</QueryErrorBoundary>
```

### ✅ DO: Use DevTools for Debugging
Enable development tools for better debugging:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: DevTools setup
import { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query'
import { ReactQueryDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-query-devtools'

export const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      // ... options
    },
  },
})

// In your App component
function App() {
  return (
    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
      {/* Your app */}
      {__DEV__ && <ReactQueryDevtools />}
    </QueryClientProvider>
  )
}
```

## File Organization

### ✅ DO: Organize by Feature
Structure your code by feature rather than by type:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Feature-based organization
// features/auth/
  // hooks/
    // useAuth.ts
    // useAuthStore.ts
  // components/
    // LoginForm.tsx
    // UserProfile.tsx
  // services/
    // authApi.ts
  // types/
    // auth.types.ts

// ❌ Bad: Type-based organization
// hooks/
  // auth/
  // components/
  // auth/
  // services/
  // auth/
```

This structure ensures that related code is co-located and easier to maintain as features grow.